  "notes": "The lane’s centre of gravity, and the only entry in this batch the research describes as five separately documented production regimes rather than one style — so the five are authored as `modes` and the top-level block is only what they share. The shared spine: 100–110 straight against 134–140 half-time, D♭ as the single most frequent centre across every regime, kick on 1 and the and-of-3 under a clap+snap 2&4, and a sliding 808 pinned to the kick with portamento between chord roots (`bass808.slideProb` 0.35, `portamentoMs` 60). The parameter that is most his is `arrangement.transitions.switchUpProb` at 0.45, by a distance the highest here — the research names the mid-song beat switch as the defining habit, not a decoration. The second is the withheld hook: one of the three structures never reaches a `hook` at all, which is what the research means by hooks “frequently withheld or replaced by a chanted refrain”. ⚠ `modes` replaces the genre’s mustard/ratchet/smooth rather than adding to them, which is correct here — GKMC, TPAB, DAMN., Mr. Morale and GNX are the moods this author actually has. ⚠ Mode B swaps the bass to `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.4 because the research says Thundercat’s six-string replaces the 808 entirely and plays running fusion lines as the melodic lead; no other mode does that. ⚠ The beat-hoarding workflow quote (“97,000 gigs of beats”, 80–100 songs cut for GNX) is not a generator parameter and is carried here rather than faked as one. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 45 to 44 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 45 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
